<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Lakes</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Lakes are five large freshwater lakes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> on the border of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. By 1710 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> in North America extended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadia">Acadia</a> and the St Lawrence through the Great Lakes and down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-lakes.jpg" alt="Great Lakes" width="320" height="246" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Lakes,_No_Clouds_(4968915002)_Brighter.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Great Lakes are a series of interconnected freshwater lakes spanning the Canada-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> border: Superior, Michigan, Huron, Erie and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>. Hydrologically, Michigan and Huron are a single body of water joined at the Straits of Mackinac. Water flows from Superior to Huron and Michigan, south to Erie and finally north to Lake Ontario, and leaves by the Saint Lawrence River to the Atlantic. The lakes hold more than 20 per cent of the world's surface fresh water, and because of their rolling waves and distant horizons they have long been called inland seas.</p><p>The lakes began to form at the end of the Last Glacial Period around 14,000 years ago, as retreating ice sheets exposed the basins they had carved and meltwater filled them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Indigenous peoples, called Paleo-indians, inhabited the region around 10,000 BCE. From around 1000 CE the peoples of the region traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> nuggets, which were fashioned into ornaments and weapons found in the mounds of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>. Several lake names come from Indigenous languages: Erie from an Iroquoian word for long tail, Michigan from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ojibwe">Ojibwe</a> mishi-gami, great water, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> from the Wendat ontarí'io, lake of shining waters. The first French explorers named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-huron">Lake Huron</a> for the Wendat.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-1812">War of 1812</a> the Rush-Bagot Treaty of 1818, and later the Treaty of Washington, eventually led to a complete disarmament of naval vessels on the lakes. Storms and reefs have sunk hundreds of ships, and the Lake Superior shipwreck coast from Grand Marais, Michigan, to Whitefish Point became known as the Graveyard of the Great Lakes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Great Lakes Waterway connects all five lakes, and the Saint Lawrence Seaway links them to the Atlantic. Most trade is in bulk goods such as iron ore, grain and potash, and large sections of the lakes freeze in winter. Major cities of the region include Chicago, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/detroit">Detroit</a>, Cleveland, Buffalo and Milwaukee on the American side and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a>, Mississauga and Hamilton on the Canadian side.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Great Lakes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Exchange networks reaching from the <b>Great Lakes</b> to the Gulf of Mexico brought copper, mica, marine shells and shark teeth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cahokia">Cahokia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>By around 1710, the first empire stretched from Acadia along the St. Lawrence through the <b>Great Lakes</b> and down the Mississippi to Louisiana, covering some ten million square kilometres.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Nkore was one of the interlacustrine kingdoms whose history shows how states formed among the herding and farming peoples of the <b>Great Lakes</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ankole">Kingdom of Ankole · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Buganda was the most centralised of the <b>Great Lakes</b> kingdoms.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-buganda">Kingdom of Buganda · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries the Babito kings were the most powerful rulers of the <b>Great Lakes</b> region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bunyoro">Kingdom of Bunyoro · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Young men went west as coureurs des bois and, later, licensed voyageurs, paddling canoes to posts on the <b>Great Lakes</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
